config USBADB_GATE
	bool "publish whether a non-dumb USB host is attached"
	depends on USB
	depends on USB_GADGET
	help
	  Publishes whether the USB gadget is configured by a real host, so that
	  Android's adb daemon can be kept running only while a PC is attached.

	  Detection is the gadget state reported by the UDC core: only
	  "configured" means a host sent SET_CONFIGURATION, which a dumb charger
	  never does.

	  THIS DRIVER RUNS NOTHING. It only publishes. The acting half is
	  patches/usbadb_gate.rc, which init starts; a child spawned from kernel
	  context cannot make init act on a setprop or a service control message
	  (measured, with SELinux permissive: every tool exits 0 and nothing
	  changes), so the kernel decides and a legitimate caller carries it out.

	  Event driven throughout: the gadget state notifier is edge triggered and
	  the hand-off node blocks its reader, so there is no polling, no timer and
	  no wakeup at all while the cable state is steady.

	  One exception, and it is deliberate: writing sys.usb.config makes
	  Android's USB HAL tear the gadget down and re-enumerate it, which the
	  notifier cannot tell from a cable pull. A verdict that differs from
	  what the reader was last told is therefore held for 3000 ms before it is
	  published, in both directions, and only the verdict standing at the end
	  of that window is sent. Armed by a single one-shot delayed work, never
	  re-armed from inside it, and cancelled when the verdict comes back to
	  what the reader already has. That one rule is the whole correction: it
	  needs no second clock, because elapsed time is the only thing that
	  separates a real change from our own echo.

	  The window is 3 s and not less, because the echo is long: teardown to
	  re-configured takes ~1.7 s on a measured device, and the reader's own
	  adb_on()/adb_off() takes up to another 0.9 s, during which the port is
	  still down. A shorter window publishes "off" in the middle of the echo
	  and the reader's own mtp write re-tears the port, which is the loop this
	  window exists to stop. If your device re-enumerates faster, this is the
	  one number worth lowering - and it is safe to lower only if the echo
	  round trip is measurably under it.

	  Hand-off: /dev/usbadb_gate (0600) returns one line per verdict change,
	      blocking until then: "on configured 7"
	  Control:  /sys/kernel/usbadb_gate/state
	      1 = enabled (default): judge the cable and publish verdicts
	      0 = disabled: publish nothing, leave the reader alone
	  Read:     /sys/kernel/usbadb_gate/link
	      "udc=configured src=event enabled=1 held=none published=on events=12"

	  There is no "force adb on" here, and no field that reports adb's
	  state, because this driver cannot start a daemon or write a property
	  and never looks at either. It publishes what the gadget reported; the
	  reader decides what to do about it.

	  Only meaningful on Android devices with a USB gadget driver.

	  If unsure, say N.

config USBADB_GATE_UDC_STATE
	string "Gadget state node"
	depends on USBADB_GATE
	default "/sys/class/udc/a600000.dwc3/state"
	help
	  sysfs node whose content says whether a host configured us.
	  "configured" means a PC; anything else ("not attached", "attached",
	  "powered", "suspend") means no usable data link, so adb stays off.

	  Find yours with:
	    for d in /sys/class/udc/*; do echo -n "$d: "; cat "$d/state"; done

	  Qualcomm SoCs are usually a600000.dwc3, MediaTek 11200000.usb.
